    {
      "@id": "references/arxiv-hep-th-9308100v1",
      "@type": [
        "fabio:Work",
        "bibo:AcademicArticle"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "Gedanken Experiments Involving Black Holes",
      "dcterms:creator": [
        "Susskind, Leonard"
      ],
      "dcterms:issued": "D:20241126",
      "dcterms:identifier": "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9308100",
      "dcterms:abstract": "Analysis of several gedanken experiments indicates that black hole complementarity cannot be ruled out on the basis of known physical principles. Experiments designed by outside observers to disprove the existence of a quantum-mechanical stretched horizon require knowledge of Planck-scale eﬀects for their analysis. Observers who fall through the event horizon after sampling the Hawking radiation cannot discover duplicate information inside the black hole before hitting the singularity. Experiments by outside observers to detect baryon number violation will yield signiﬁcant eﬀects well outside the stretched horizon. PACS categories: 04.60.+n, 12.25.+e, 97.60.Lf ⋆susskind@dormouse.stanford.edu † larus@dormouse.stanford.edu 1",
      "dcterms:source": "library/arxiv-hep-th-9308100v1/manifest.jsonld",
      "dcterms:hasVersion": "library/arxiv-hep-th-9308100v1"
    },
    {
      "@id": "references/susskind-thorlacius1993",
      "@type": [
        "fabio:Work",
        "bibo:Article"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "Gedanken Experiments Involving Black Holes",
      "dcterms:creator": [
        "Susskind, Leonard",
        "Thorlacius, Larus"
      ],
      "dcterms:identifier": "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9308100"
    },
    {
      "@id": "references/arxiv-hep-th-9306069v2",
      "@type": [
        "fabio:Work",
        "bibo:AcademicArticle"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "The stretched horizon and black hole complementarity",
      "dcterms:creator": [
        "Susskind, Leonard"
      ],
      "dcterms:issued": "D:20241126",
      "dcterms:identifier": "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9306069",
      "dcterms:abstract": "Three postulates asserting the validity of conventional quantum theory, semi-classical general relativity and the statistical basis for thermodynamics are introduced as a foundation for the study of black hole evolution. We explain how these postulates may be implemented in a “stretched horizon” or membrane description of the black hole, appropriate to a distant observer. The technical analysis is illustrated in the simpliﬁed context of 1+1 dimensional dilaton gravity. Our postulates imply that the dissipative properties of the stretched horizon arise from a course graining of microphysical degrees of freedom that the horizon must possess. A principle of black hole complementarity is advocated. The overall viewpoint is similar to that pioneered by ’t Hooft but the detailed implementation is diﬀerent. PACS categories: 04.60.+n, 12.25.+e, 97.60.Lf ⋆susskind@dormouse.stanford.edu † larus@dormouse.stanford.edu ‡ john@dormouse.stanford.edu 1",
      "dcterms:source": "library/arxiv-hep-th-9306069v2/manifest.jsonld",
      "dcterms:hasVersion": "library/arxiv-hep-th-9306069v2"
    },
    {
      "@id": "references/arxiv-1810.11563v1",
      "@type": [
        "fabio:Work",
        "bibo:AcademicArticle"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "Three Lectures on Complexity and Black Holes",
      "dcterms:creator": [
        "Susskind, Leonard"
      ],
      "dcterms:issued": "D:20181030",
      "dcterms:identifier": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.11563",
      "dcterms:abstract": "This is the written version of three lectures on complexity and black holes given at PiTP 2018 summer program entitled ”From Qubits to Spacetime.” The ﬁrst lecture describes the meaning of quantum complexity, the analogy between entropy and complexity, and the second law of complexity. Lecture two reviews the connection between the second law of complexity and the interior of black holes. I discuss how ﬁrewalls are related to periods of non-increasing complexity which typically only occur after an exponentially long time. The ﬁnal lecture is about the thermodynamics of complexity, and “uncomplexity” as a resource for doing computational work. I explain the remarkable power of “one clean qubit,” in both computational terms and in space-time terms. The lectures can also be found online at https://static.ias.edu/pitp/2018/ node/1796.html . arXiv:1810.11563v1 [hep-th] 27 Oct 2018",
      "dcterms:source": "library/arxiv-1810.11563v1/manifest.jsonld",
      "dcterms:hasVersion": "library/arxiv-1810.11563v1"
    },
