    {
      "@id": "references/szangolies",
      "@type": [
        "fabio:Work",
        "bibo:Article"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "The standard model symmetry and qubit entanglement",
      "dcterms:creator": [
        "Szangolies, Jochen"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "references/5s032x",
      "@type": [
        "fabio:Work",
        "bibo:AcademicArticle"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "The Standard Model Symmetry and Qubit Entanglement",
      "dcterms:creator": [
        "Szangolies∗, Jochen"
      ],
      "dcterms:issued": "D:20230605",
      "bibo:doi": "10.32388/5S032X",
      "dcterms:identifier": "https://doi.org/10.32388/5S032X",
      "dcterms:abstract": "quantum description. Indeed, the links appear so deep that Susskind has proposed to generalize ER = EPR, the conjecture that quantum entanglement is equivalent to wormholes in spacetime [6], to GR = QM, proposing that ‘where there is quantum mechanics, there is also gravity’ [7]. Hence, a quantum system does not merely come equipped with a geometry, but that geometry is dynamical, approximating general relativistic spacetimes in a classical limit. The origin of this idea can be traced to work of Jacobson, who has shown that Einstein’s equations can be recovered from thermodynamics in the presence of black hole horizons [8] (see also [9, 10]). These arguments rely essentially on the area law-scaling of the Bekenstein-Hawking entropy of black holes [11]. This scaling is also observed, in many cases, for the entanglement entropy of quantum systems—indeed, it has been proposed to explain black hole entropy as the entanglement entropy of degrees of freedom across the horizon [12, 13]. Along a different thread, van Raamsdonk has suggested that entanglement may be responsible for spacetime connec- tivity [14, 15]. In the AdS/CFT correspondence, the Ryu-Takayanagi formula [16] relates the entanglement between different patches of the conformal field theory on the boundary of Anti-de Sitter space with the area of a surface partitioning the AdS-space; if the entanglement between both goes to zero, the space partitions into two disconnected regions. Combining these perspectives, the linearized Einstein equations follow from ‘entanglement thermodynamics’ for perturbations of the AdS-spacetime [17, 18]. While the bulk of this work assumes the AdS/CFT-correspondence, Jacobson has shown that a derivation of the full Einstein equations is possible without assuming an AdS-background, given a condition of entanglement equilibrium [19]. A way to think about this is in terms of a graph, each node of which is itself an abstract element of quantum information—a qubit, or perhaps more gen",
      "dcterms:source": "library/5s032x/manifest.jsonld",
      "dcterms:hasVersion": "library/5s032x"
    },
    {
      "@id": "references/arxiv-1910.09966v1",
      "@type": [
        "fabio:Work",
        "bibo:AcademicArticle"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "Braids, 3-manifolds, elementary particles: number theory and symmetry in particle physics",
      "dcterms:creator": [
        "T. Asslmeyer-Maluga (German Aerospace Center, Berlin)"
      ],
      "dcterms:issued": "D:20191023",
      "dcterms:identifier": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.09966",
      "dcterms:abstract": "In this paper, we will describe a topological model for elementary par- ticles based on 3-manifolds. Here, we will use Thurston’s geometrization theorem to get a simple picture: fermions as hyperbolic knot comple- ments (a complement C(K) = S3 \\ (K × D2) of a knot K carrying a hyperbolic geometry) and bosons as torus bundles. In particular, hyper- bolic 3-manifolds have a close connection to number theory (Bloch group, algebraic K-theory, quaternionic trace ﬁelds), which will be used in the description of fermions. Here, we choose the description of 3-manifolds by branched covers. Every 3-manifold can be described by a 3-fold branched cover of S3 branched along a knot. In case of knot complements, one will obtain a 3-fold branched cover of the 3-disk D3 branched along a 3-braid or 3-braids describing fermions. The whole approach will un- cover new symmetries as induced by quantum and discrete groups. Using the Drinfeld–Turaev quantization, we will also construct a quantization so that quantum states correspond to knots. Particle properties like the electric charge must be expressed by topology, and we will obtain the right spectrum of possible values. Finally, we will get a connection to recent models of Furey, Stoica and Gresnigt using octonionic and quaternionic algebras with relations to 3-braids (Bilson–Thompson model).",
      "dcterms:source": "library/arxiv-1910.09966v1/manifest.jsonld",
      "dcterms:hasVersion": "library/arxiv-1910.09966v1"
    },
